    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.22715_p6_b61",
      "text": "Motion Language Multi-Task Contrastive Instruction Tuning. Motion language model pre-\ntraining teaches the LLM to read IMU token sequences, but next-token prediction alone does not\nprovide the discriminative motion-language alignment needed by retrieval and zero-shot recognition.\nAt the same time, captioning still requires the model to preserve its generative capability. We therefore\nuse multi-task contrastive instruction tuning to jointly support embedding-based and generation-based\nmotion-language tasks [45, 75], as illustrated in Figure 4.\n",
      "bbox": [
        107.64,
        586.8,
        505.74,
        651.47
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        430,
        2347,
        2022,
        2605
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 548,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.22715_p6_b62",
      "text": "The language supervision comes from Nymeria [40] atomic-action annotations: we use the atomic-\naction text as motion narration and derive activity-label names from our semi-automatically curated\nand human-verified ground-truth action labels. To increase linguistic diversity, each motion window\nis paired with five augmented narrations, as detailed in Section A.4. For each training example,\nthe IMU branch wraps the token sequence s in a prompt that also describes the visible wearable\nlocations, while the language side contains two supervision branches built on the same text pooler: a\n",
      "bbox": [
        107.69,
        657.87,
        505.65,
        722.39
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
